      <h1>{frontmatter.title}</h1>
      <time>{frontmatter.date}</time>

      <p class="intro">How to play Splendor, in one breath: on your turn you do one of three things. Take gem tokens, reserve a card you want, or spend gems to buy a card. Every card you buy is a permanent discount on every card after it, and the first player to 15 points sets off the final round. It seats 2 to 4 people, runs about 30 minutes, and most tables are playing inside five minutes.</p>

      <p>I have taught this game at least fifty times, at game nights, at a bar, to my own parents. The rules are never the hard part. The hard part is that a lot of how-to pages repeat a rule that does not exist, and one quiet line in the book decides every close game. This walkthrough follows the copy you actually own.</p>

      <p>Below is the whole game in the order it clicks. What is in the box, setup, your turn, how the gem engine really works, nobles, the rule most tables get wrong, and the tiebreaker that flips the entire strategy on its head.</p>

      <h2>What Is in the Box</h2>
      <p>You get 90 development cards split into three levels: 40 cards in level one, 30 in level two, 20 in level three. The higher the level, the more a card costs and the more points it carries.</p>

      <p>Then the gems. 40 tokens in all. Seven each of five colors (emerald green, sapphire blue, ruby red, diamond white, onyx black), plus five gold tokens that act as wilds. Gems are not yours forever. They are a wallet you spend and refill, not a collection you keep.</p>

      <p>Last, 10 noble tiles. They sit face up in the middle and pay out bonus points when your collection matches their taste, which we will get to.</p>

      <p>Each development card shows three things: its cost in the bottom left, the permanent gem bonus it gives you in the top right, and its points in the top left. No number in the top left means it is worth zero points. Plenty of good cards are worth zero points, and that is not a mistake.</p>

      <h2>Setup in 90 Seconds</h2>
      <p>Shuffle each level separately and stack them in a column, level one at the bottom, level three on top. Then flip four cards face up from each deck and lay them in a line next to their stack. You should see a grid of three rows, four cards each.</p>

      <p>Shuffle the nobles and reveal a number of them equal to the player count plus one. The rest go back in the box. Sort the gem tokens into piles and you are done.</p>

      <p>One thing changes with the table size, and it matters more than it looks:</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
